deep.reconstruction:
There is no mathematics on this page: no formulas, computations or derivations. The page is part of a set of German lecture or study notes on early Greek history, continuing from the previous page's section 'Hauptmomente zu der frühsten Bildung der Nation'. It lists early joint enterprises of the Greeks, in a chronological scheme typical of Göttingen ancient-history teaching around 1800 (Heeren-style handbooks).

1. The voyage of the Argonauts, c. 1250 BC. It is called the first Greek voyage into the Black Sea. Leaders of several states took part. It set out from Thessaly under Jason and was most probably a raid for plunder.

2. The war of the Seven against Thebes, c. 1225 BC, waged by seven allied princes. They achieved nothing and all but one died (traditionally Adrastus is the survivor). Ten years later their sons, the Epigoni, took Thebes and destroyed it. The '10' is an interlinear insertion.

3. The Trojan War, c. 1200 BC (date inserted above the line). It is described as the first great national undertaking of the Greeks:
   - all Hellenic peoples took part, each under its own king;
   - Agamemnon was the common supreme leader (name inserted in the margin);
   - the army numbered 100,000 men and the fleet 1,000 ships;
   - for 9 years they fought Troy's allies, and in the 10th year they besieged Troy and took it by a ruse.

4. The last line begins a summary sentence about the heroic age. It continues on the next page: '...[waren] die Griechen noch genau so mit einander verbunden.'

The only arithmetic is trivial date bookkeeping: Epigoni c. 1225 − 10 = c. 1215 BC, and 9 years of fighting plus the 10th-year siege. The handwriting and content suggest notes taken by someone, not necessarily Gauss himself, filed in this bundle.
